WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.057
In Postgres, we do not need a special pivot keyword for the patterns in this module.

00:00:05.057 --> 00:00:08.132
Instead, we can use conditional aggregation.

00:00:08.132 --> 00:00:12.622
One common method uses a case expression inside an aggregate.

00:00:12.622 --> 00:00:19.967
Postgres also provides the filter clause, which often makes conditional counts and sums easier to read.

00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:28.052
Both methods follow the same idea: group the rows, then calculate a separate aggregate for each category we want to

00:00:28.052 --> 00:00:29.057
display as a column.
